# /etc/rancher/k3s/registries.yaml  — MANAGED BY ANSIBLE
#
# Routes ghcr.io pulls through the in-cluster pull-through cache
# (deploy/infra/registry/) so a layer is fetched from the internet once and
# served inside the cluster to every other node.
#
# The endpoint is a literal ClusterIP, not a DNS name, because containerd runs
# on the host and resolves through the host resolver, never CoreDNS. A ClusterIP
# is still reachable from the host: kube-proxy programs its rules for
# host-originated traffic too. This address is pinned by the mirror's Service
# and must not drift.
#
# ghcr.io is listed SECOND on purpose. containerd walks the endpoint list in
# order and falls through on failure, so a missing or unhealthy mirror silently
# degrades to pulling from ghcr.io, which is exactly the behaviour that exists
# today. Nothing here can make a pull fail that would otherwise have succeeded.
#
# Only ghcr.io is mirrored. A distribution registry proxies exactly one upstream,
# and the fleet's own images are the ones that actually churn; docker.io and
# registry.k8s.io continue to be pulled directly.
#
# NOTE: k3s reads this file at startup and renders it into
# /var/lib/rancher/k3s/agent/etc/containerd/certs.d/. Changing it requires a
# k3s-agent restart to take effect.
mirrors:
  ghcr.io:
    endpoint:
      - "http://{{ registry_mirror_endpoint }}"
      - "https://ghcr.io"
